# Low-Temperature In Situ Amino Functionalization of TiO[!sub]2[!/sub] Nanoparticles Sharpens Electron Management Achieving over 21% Efficient Planar Perovskite Solar Cells

## abstract
Titanium oxide (TiO2) has been commonly used as an electron transport layer (ETL) of regular-structure perovskite solar cells (PSCs), and so far the reported PSC devices with power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) over 21% are mostly based on mesoporous structures containing an indispensable mesoporous TiO2 layer. However, a high temperature annealing (over 450 degrees C) treatment is mandatory, which is incompatible with low-cost fabrication and flexible devices. Herein, a facile one-step, low-temperature, nonhydrolytic approach to in situ synthesizing amino-functionalized TiO2 nanoparticles (abbreviated as NH2-TiO2 NPs) is developed by chemical bonding of amino (-NH2) groups, via Ti-N bonds, onto the surface of TiO2 NPs. NH2-TiO2 NPs are then incorporated as an efficient ETL in n-i-p planar heterojunction (PHJ) PSCs, affording PCE over 21%. Cs(0.05)FA(0.83)MA(0.12)PbI(2.55)Br(0.45) (abbreviated as CsFAMA) PHJ PSC devices based on NH2-TiO2 ETL exhibit the best PCE of 21.33%, which is significantly higher than that of the devices based on the pristine TiO2 ETL (19.82%) and is close to the record PCE for devices with similar structures and fabrication procedures. Besides, due to the passivation of the surface trap states of perovskite film, the hysteresis of current-voltage response is significantly suppressed, and the ambient stability of devices is improved upon amino functionalization.

Low-Temperature In Situ Amino Functionalization of TiO2 Nanoparticles Sharpens Electron Management Achieving over 21% Efficient Planar Perovskite Solar Cells
Titanium oxide $\left(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right)$ has been commonly used as an electron transport layer (ETL) of regular-structure perovskite solar cells (PSCs), and so far the reported PSC devices with power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) over $27\%$ are mostly based on mesoporous structures containing an indispensable mesoporous $\bar{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ layer. However, a high temperature annealing (over $450^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ treatment is mandatory, which is incompatible with low-cost fabrication and flexible devices. Herein, a facile one-step, low-temperature, nonhydrolytic approach to in situ synthesizing amino-functionalized $\bar{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ nanoparticles (abbreviated as ${\mathsf{N H}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ NPs) is developed by chemical bonding of amino $(\mathsf{-N H}_{2})$ groups, via TiN bonds, onto the surface of $\bar{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ NPs. ${\mathsf{N H}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ NPs are then incorporated as an efficient ETL in n-i-p planar heterojunction (PHJ) PSCs, affording PCE over $27\%$ . $\mathsf{C s}_{0.05}\mathsf{F A}_{0.83}\mathsf{M A}_{0.12}\mathsf{P b}\mathsf{I}_{2.55}\mathsf{B r}_{0.45}$ (abbreviated as CsFAMA) PHJ PSC devices based on $N H_{2}\cdot T i O_{2}$ ETL exhibit the best PCE of $21.33\%$ , which is significantly higher than that of the devices based on the pristine $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ ETL $(79.82\%)$ and is close to the record PCE for devices with similar structures and fabrication procedures. Besides, due to the passivation of the surface trap states of perovskite film, the hysteresis of current–voltage response is significantly suppressed, and the ambient stability of devices is improved upon amino functionalization.
Organic–inorganic hybrid perovskite materials exemplified by such organometal halides as $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{X}_{3}$ $(\mathrm{X}~=~\mathrm{I})$ Br, and Cl) have been attracting intense interests as light-absorbing materials of solar cells owing to their large absorption coefficients, small exciton binding energies, high carrier mobilities, tunable bandgaps, and ease of fabrication, rendering organic–inorganic hybrid perovskite solar cells (PSCs) as a promising alternative to the commercialized crystalline-Si and inorganic semiconductor thin film solar cells.[1] During the past few years, tremendous efforts on optimizing the device structure and engineering the perovskite/electrode interfaces as well as the composition, phase, and morphology of the perovskite layer have pushed the power conversion efficiency (PCE) ever increasing to over 23%.[2]
So far the reported PSC devices with PCE over $21\%$ are mostly based on mesoporous structures containing an indispensable mesoporous titanium oxide $\left(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right)$ layer, which requires a high temperature sintering (over $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ treatment and is thus incompatible with low-cost fabrication and flexible devices.[3] Alternatively, planar heterojunction (PHJ) PSCs typically based on a substituting compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer composed of colloidal nanoparticles, which can be fabricated by low-temperature (generally $\mathrm{<}150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ processing, have been investigated extensively, showing the advantages of simpler device structures and compatibility with flexible substrates.[4] In particular, for the regular-structure (n-i-p) PHJ PSC devices, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ sandwiched between the bottom transparent electrode and the perovskite layer atop has been commonly used as an electron transport layer (ETL), and shows efficient electron transport property with the advantage of low-cost and phase stability. However, without the high-temperature sintering treatment, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL synthesized at low temperature suffers from relatively low electrical conductivity and electron mobility, poor crystallinity, and high density of electronic trap states,[4b,5] resulting in low PCE with severe hysteresis of current–voltage response and inferior long-term stability of the device.[6] In order to improve the electron transport property of low-temperature solution-processed $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL, morphology optimization,[7] doping,[8] and surface modifications[4b,9,10] have been developed. In specific, the latter approach appears facile and has been thus extensively reported recently.[4b,9,10] For instance, Zhan and co-workers deposited an amino-substituted perylene diimide polymer on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL, which modulated the work function of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ matching with the conduction band of the perovskite, leading to efficient charge extraction.[10] Likewise, amino acids had been used to modify the high-temperature treated mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer, beneficial for passivating the surface traps at $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/C H_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ interface and promoting the crystal growth of $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ perovskite with preferential orientation.[11] These reports reveal that the amino groups of the modifiers may effectively passivate the surface traps at $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/C H_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ interface. Note that, nevertheless, in these reports the amino groups were involved via the surface modification strategy by spin-coating the modifier onto $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL, thus rendering relatively weak interactions between the amino groups and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface only. Besides, incorporation of the additional modifier layer would inevitably increase the device fabrication complexity and cost. Therefore, it is highly desired to modify $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL in the course of its preparation (i.e., in situ) by the amino groups with reinforced interactions, ideally via chemical bonding.
Herein, we report a facile one-step, low-temperature, nonhydrolytic approach to in situ synthesize amino-functionalized $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles (abbreviated as ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}–{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ NPs) by doping urea as a precursor during the alcoholysis of $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ , featuring in bonding of amino $\left(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ groups via $\mathrm{Ti-N}$ bonds onto the surface of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs. Amino functionalization of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs not only lowers the work function of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ but also decreases the defects at the surface of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . Upon applying ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ NPs as a novel ETL, regular-structure $\mathrm{Cs_{0.05}F A_{0.83}M A_{0.12}P b I_{2.55}B r_{0.45}}$ (abbreviated as CsFAMA) PHJ PSC devices exhibit significant PCE enhancement relative to the devices based on the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL. The effects of amino functionalization on the work function of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , the wettability of perovskite layer atop $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$
ETL, as well as the crystallinity and surface trap states of perovskite film are investigated systematically, and the mechanism responsible for the efficiency enhancement is proposed.
Pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs were synthesized by a nonhydrolytic sol– gel method reported in literatures.[4a,8] To prepare ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ NPs, urea was doped into a $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ solution dissolved in anhydrous ethanol as a precursor with variable N:Ti molar ratios of $3\%$ , $10\%$ , and $20\%$ (abbreviated as $3\%{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}{\mathrm{-TiO}}_{2}$ $10\%\mathrm{-NH}_{2}.$ - $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $20\%{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , respectively). After the alcoholysis of $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ in anhydrous benzyl alcohol at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , titanium(diisopropoxide) bis(2,4-pentanedionate) (TIPD) was added to disperse and stabilize the as-prepared ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ NPs. Figure 1a,b shows the transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images of the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $20\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}}$ NPs, indicating quasi-monodispersed particles. The corresponding highresolution (HR) TEM images are illustrated in Figure  1c,d, in which the lattice fringes of (101) plane with space distance of ${\approx}3.5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ are clearly observed, showing $20\%{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ with sizes of 9.2 and $9.7\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. According to their selected area electron diffraction (SAED) patterns, both $20\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}}$ and pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs are polycrystalline anatase, and the relatively brighter polymorphic ring observed for $20\%{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ suggests its higher crystallinity.[12] These results are well consistent with the reported $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs prepared by the same method.[4a,8] According to the X-ray diffraction (XRD) characterization, the diffraction patterns of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}–{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ NPs and the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs match well with the standard pattern of crystalline anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (JCPDS:21-1272, see Figure  S1 in the Supporting Information). All of the diffraction peaks of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ NPs exhibit higher intensities than those of the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs, suggesting that the crystallinity of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs is improved after amino functionalization. Besides, the crystal size of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs estimated by Scherrer’s equation decreases slightly after amino functionalization with the increase of the molar ratio of urea precursor in the starting solution (see Table S1 in the Supporting Information). This phenomenon is understandable since more urea in the starting precursor solution leads to the formation of more nuclei at the beginning of alcoholysis process; thus, smaller $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ crystals would form.[13] The decrease of the crystal size is beneficial for dispersion of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs in ethanol as confirmed from the dynamic light scattering (DLS) measurements (see Figure  S2 in the Supporting Information), and this further facilitates the film formation of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL as discussed below.
In order to clarify the influence of urea doped in the precursor solution on the chemical structure of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs, we carried out Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) and X-ray photo­ electron spectroscopic (XPS) characterizations. Figure  1e,f compares the FT-IR spectra of the $20\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}}$ , pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and urea precursor. For the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , a broad vibrational band in the region of $400{-}800~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ with two characteristic vibrational peaks at ${\approx}423$ and $446~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ assigned to the stretching vibration modes of $_{\mathrm{Ti-O-Ti}}$ bonds is observed, while the intense peak at $1637\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is attributed to the bending vibration of $_\mathrm{O-H}$ bonds, for which the stretching vibrations are observed as a broad band in the region o $\mathrm{92}{\approx}3150{-}3450\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ (see also Figure S3 in the Supporting Information).[14] Several characteristic vibrational peaks are observed in the FT-IR spectrum of urea, including the stretching (3453, $3345~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ ), scissoring $(1598~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ ) vibrations and rocking mode $(1154~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ ) of the - ${\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ group, stretching vibrations of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ bonds $(1684~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ ), and stretching vibrations of $\mathrm{C-N}$ bonds $(1467~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ ).[15] In the FT-IR spectrum of $20\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}}$ , in addition to the characteristic vibrational peaks of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\scriptstyle\left(\approx423\right.$ and $446~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}.$ ) and $_{\mathrm{O-H}}$ bonds $(1637~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ), several new vibrational peaks at 405, 488, 1161, and $1570~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are detected. While the latter two peaks at 1570 and $1161~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are assigned to the scissoring mode and rocking mode of - $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group despite some shifts relative to those observed in the FT-IR spectrum of urea,[16] the appearance of the new peaks at 405 and $488~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (see Figure 1f) indicates the formation of new chemical bonds, presumably $\mathrm{Ti-N}$ bonds.[17]
Shown in Figure 1g is the high-resolution N1s XPS spectra of $20\%{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and urea precursor. Upon amino functionalization, N1s signal appears obviously according to the comparison of the XPS survey spectra of $20\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}}$ and pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (see Figure  S4a,d in the Supporting Information), which can be deconvoluted into three peaks centered at 399.6, 400.3, and $401.3\ \mathrm{eV}.$ The latter two peaks can be assigned to $\gamma\mathrm{-N}$ state (chemsorbed N-containing species such as $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ ), and the former one corresponds to - $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ moiety,[18] for which the relative intensity to the chemsorbed N-containing species increases with the increase of the molar ratio of urea precursor in the starting solution (see Figure  S5 in the Supporting Information). It is noteworthy that such a - $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ signal at $399.6\mathrm{eV}$ shifts negatively by $0.2~\mathrm{eV}$ relative to that within urea $(399.8\ \mathrm{eV})$ , suggesting the increase of negative charge of N atom within $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group. This confirms the formation of new $_{\mathrm{Ti-N}}$ bonds affording the increase of negative charge of N atom within - $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group. Nevertheless, no discernible change in the Ti2p signals in the XPS spectra of $20\%{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is observed (see Figure S4b in the Supporting Information), suggesting that the content of the as-formed $_{\mathrm{Ti-N}}$ bonds is relatively low.[19]
An intriguing question is whether the detected - $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ signals in both FT-IR and XPS spectra of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}–{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ originated from the urea precursor or its decomposed species. According to the comparison of the N1s XPS spectra of $20\%{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and urea precursor, the - $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ signal at $399.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ shifts negatively by $0.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ relative to that observed in the spectrum of urea $(399.8\ \mathrm{eV},$ see Figure  1g), suggesting that the urea precursor decomposed completely during the alcoholysis of $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ .[18a,b] The decomposition of urea in the final ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}–{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ NPs is further confirmed by analyzing its C1s XPS spectra, revealing that the characteristic $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ signal at $287.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ observed for urea is absent in the spectrum of $20\%{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (see Figure $\mathtt{S4c}$ in the Supporting Information);[20] thus, the retaining of urea within $20\%{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ can be ruled out. This conclusion is solidified by the FT-IR results showing the disappearance of the characteristic vibrational signals at $1684~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $\scriptstyle(\mathsf{C}=\mathsf{O})$ and $1467~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $(\mathsf{C}-\mathsf{N})$ of urea in the FT-IR spectrum of $20\%{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (see Figure  1e). Therefore, the $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ signal detected in both FT-IR and XPS spectra should result from the decomposition of urea precursor, affording ammonia and the intermediate cyanate $(\mathrm{OCN^{-}})$ , which were absorbed on the Lewis acid sites of the surface of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and then transformed to $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups by heat treatment.[13]
The effect of amino functionalization on the valence states of Ti and O elements within $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is further examined by XPS spectroscopy. According to the comparison of the Ti2p XPS spectra of the $20\%{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , the $\mathrm{Ti}2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ and $\mathrm{Ti}2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ peaks with the binding energies of 464.6 and $458.9\ \mathrm{eV}_{\mathrm{i}}$ respectively, keep almost constant without discernible shifts (see Figure S4b in the Supporting Information), indicating that amino functionalization has negligible influence on the valence state of Ti. On the contrary, the O1s XPS spectra of the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and the $20\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}}$ show dramatic difference (Figure 1h,i). For the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , the O1s signal can be deconvoluted into three peaks centered at ${\approx}530.1$ , 531.0, and $532.3\ \mathrm{eV},$ which can be assigned to the ${{\mathrm O}^{2-}}$ anion bonded with the $\mathrm{Ti^{4+}}$ cation in the stoichiometric $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ structure (labeled as $\mathrm{~\cal{O}_{M})~}$ , the oxygen deficiency (labeled as $|\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{V}}|$ ), and the hydroxyl groups on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (labeled as ${\mathrm{O}}_{\mathrm{OH}},$ ), respectively.[21] After amino functionalization, apparently the relative intensities of the latter two peaks decrease significantly. This result is consistent with the incorporation of - $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups substituting the hydroxyl groups. In specific, the relative content of oxygen deficiency $(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{V}})$ within $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , which is resulted from oxygen vacancy mainly,[21] estimated from the relative area of the $\mathrm{\DeltaO_{v}}$ signal $[\mathrm{O_{V}}/$ $\left(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{M}}+\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{V}}+\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{OH}}\right)]$ decreases from $10.2\%$ to $7.7\%$ , revealing the significant decrease of the oxygen vacancy. In contrast, the relative area of the $\mathrm{~\mathsf~{~O~}~}_{\mathrm{M}}$ signal $[\mathrm{O_{M}}/(\mathrm{O_{M}}+\mathrm{O_{V}}+\mathrm{O_{OH}})]$ increases dramatically from $35.4\%$ to $64.2\%$ , consistent with the decrease of the oxygen-related defects (see Table S2 in the Supporting Information). Since the defects at the surface of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film are sensitively detrimental to its conductivity, the decrease of the defects upon amino functionalization is expected to improve the conductivity of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film, as confirmed below.
The effects of amino functionalization on the optical and electrical properties and the work function of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ were then investigated systematically. The optical bandgap of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs estimated from the diffuse reflectance spectra increases from $3.11~\mathrm{eV}$ (pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}^{\cdot}$ to $3.13\ \mathrm{eV}$ $(3\%\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2})$ ) upon amino functionalization, and keeps on increasing gradually with the increase of the molar ratio of urea precursor in the starting solution (see Figure  S6 in the Supporting Information). The conductivity of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film measured from four probe method increases from $1.94\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~m}^{-1}$ (pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}^{\cdot}$ to $4.34\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~m}^{-1}$ $(3\%\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2})$ and $7.56\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~m}^{-1}$ $(10\%\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2})$ ) (see Figure  S7 and Table S3 in the Supporting Information). This can be interpreted by the decrease of the defects of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film and the increase of the crystallinity upon amino functionalization as discussed above. However, a further increase of the molar ratio of urea precursor in the starting solution to $20\%$ $(20\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-TiO_{2}}})$ results in a decrease of the conductivity since the $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group is insulating; thus, its overloading at the surface of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs is detrimental for conductivity.
Since ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}–{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ NPs are to be applied as ETL in the PHJ PSC devices, its film formation ability, transmittance, and wettability should be examined. We evaluated the film formation ability of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ NPs by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and atomic force microscopy (AFM). According to the comparison of the SEM images of the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , $3\%.\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $10\%\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ , and $20\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}}$ NPs, upon amino functionalization, the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ films look more compact with less pinholes than the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film (Figure 2a–d). This is confirmed by AFM measurements, indicating that the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}.$ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ films have smoother surfaces than the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , as evidenced from the decreases of the root-mean-square (RMS) roughness from $11.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ (pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2};$ ) to 10.8, 7.5, and $9.3\ \mathrm{nm}$ for $3\%{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}{\mathrm{-TiO}}_{2}$ , $10\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-TiO_{2}}}$ , and $20\%{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , respectively (Figure  2e–h). The smoother surface is beneficial for improving the interfacial contact between the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL and perovskite layer,[22] and $10\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}}$ with the smallest RMS roughness is expected to afford the best interfacial contact. Such a compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film can ensure considerable optical transmittance simultaneously. While the deposition of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer changes the overall optical transmittance spectrum of indium tin oxide (ITO) substrate since $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer acts as an optical spacer enabling a redistribution of the light intensity,[23] amino functionalization has negligible influence on the optical transmittance of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film (see Figure  S8 in the Supporting Information).
The influence of amino functionalization on the wettability of $\mathrm{Cs_{0.05}F A_{0.83}M A_{0.12}P b I_{2.55}B r_{0.45}}$ perovskite solution on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film is examined by contact angle measurements. The contact angle of a $4~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ droplet of CsFAMA perovskite solution on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film decreases dramatically from $15^{\circ}$ (pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\mathrm{,}$ ) to ${\approx}4^{\circ}$ ( $10\%\mathrm{-NH}_{2}.$ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2})$ and $2^{\circ}$ $(20\%\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2})$ (see Figure S9 in the Supporting Information). Thus, amino functionalization improves the wettability of perovskite precursor on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film, and this benefits the film formation of the perovskite layer atop via lowering the surface energy for heterogeneous nucleation.[3,24] Indeed, as seen from the SEM images of the CsFAMA perovskite films spin-coated on pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $10\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}}$ layers (Figure  2i,j), both perovskite films are uniform and dense without obvious pinholes, and the average crystalline grain size increases from ${\approx}294{}$ (pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2})$ to ${\approx}323~\mathrm{nm}$ $(10\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-TiO_{2}}})$ ) (see Figure  S10 in the Supporting Information). Besides, the perovskite film deposited on $10\%{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer is smoother than that on the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer according to its relatively smaller RMS roughness $(5.8\mathrm{nm})$ ) (Figure $2\mathrm{k},1$ ).
The dependence of the crystallographic structure of the CsFAMA perovskite film on the bottom $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer was studied by using synchrotron-based grazing incidence X-ray diffraction (GIXRD). Figure 3a–d compares the 2D-GIXRD patterns of the CsFAMA perovskite films deposited on different $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layers at X-ray incident angles of $0.2^{\circ}$ with surface sensitivity. The diffraction profiles are similar to those reported from highly textured crystallized perovskite films, and the scattered rings at $q\approx10$ , 20, and $22.2\ \mathrm{nm}^{-1}$ corresponding to (001), (002), and (012) diffractions of perovskite crystal, respectively.[25] Compared to perovskite deposited on the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer, the perovskite films deposited on ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}–{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ layers exhibit brighter and sharper scattered rings, indicating the improved crystallization. This conclusion is solidified by comparing the XRD patterns of the CsFAMA perovskite films deposited on different $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layers (Figure  3e), indicating the gradual increase of the intensity of the (001) diffraction plane.[26] Thus, amino functionalization of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs improves the crystallinity of perovskite film atop. On the contrary, the scattered rings assigned to $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ become weaker in the GIXRD patterns of the CsFAMA perovskite films deposited on ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ layers, revealing that amino functionalization can effectively inhibit the formation of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ . This is confirmed by comparing the azimuthally integrated intensity around the ring at $q\approx10~\mathrm{nm^{-1}}$ corresponding to the (001) diffraction plane as plotted in Figure  3f. Clearly, for the CsFAMA perovskite films deposited on the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer, the intense peak at $q\approx10\ \mathrm{nm^{-1}}$ [(001) diffraction plane] exhibits asymmetric profiles consisting of two or even more overlapped components, suggesting its composition of multiple phases.[27] After amino functionalization, that characteristic diffraction peak gradually becomes stronger and sharper with the increase of the molar ratio of urea precursor in the starting solution; as a result, the peaks observed for the films deposited on the $10\%{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $20\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}}$ layers look more symmetric. These results suggest that the crystallinity and phase purity of the CsFAMA perovskite film deposited on the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ layer are improved. Besides, the intensity ratio of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ to perovskite (001) diffraction plane decreases gradually with the increase of the molar ratio of urea precursor in the starting solution (see Table S4 in the Supporting Information), quantitatively confirming that the formation of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ phase is inhibited. Since the existence of excessive $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ in CsFAMA perovskite active layer may deteriorate device performance,[28] the inhibited formation of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ phase is expected to benefit the device performance, as discussed further below. A plausible explanation to the improved crystallinity of perovskite films after amino functionalization includes: 1) the $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups bonded on the surface of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs offer the heterogeneous nucleation sites beneficial for nucleation, and 2) the - $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups may coordinate via the lone-pair electrons of the nitrogen atoms with the $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ in the precursor as discussed below, leading to retarded perovskite crystal growth. Similar phenomena that the amino groups within the surface modifier may provide nucleation sites for crystallization of perovskite and affect the direction of its crystal growth have been reported in literatures.[11a,29]
We next incorporated ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ NPs as a novel ETL of PHJ PSC devices so as to examine the effect of amino functionalization on the device performance. We fabricated the regular-structure (n-i-p) PHJ PSC devices with the structure of $\mathrm{ITO}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ /CsFAMA perovskite/spiro-OMeTAD/Au (see Figure  4a), in which the CsFAMA perovskite layer with a thickness of ${\approx}450\mathrm{nm}$ was deposited uniformly on a compact ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ layer with a thickness of ${\approx}50~\mathrm{nm}$ , and a $200\mathrm{nm}$ thick spiro-OMeTAD $(2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}$ -tetrakis(N,N-di- $p$ -methoxyphenylamine)-9,9′- spirobifluorene) as the hole transport material (HTM) covered the perovskite layer (see Figure 4b). The photocurrent–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves of the champion devices based on different $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs measured under irradiation of $100\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ (AM 1.5G) are compared in Figure 4c. The measured photovoltaic parameters based on the statistical data of 50 devices fabricated independently, including open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ , short-circuit current $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ , fill factor (FF), power conversion efficiency (PCE), series resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{s}}\right)$ , and shunt resistance $(R_{\mathrm{sh}})$ , are summarized in Table  1. The control device based on the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL exhibits a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.16~\mathrm{V},$ a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $23.39\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , a FF of $73.05\%$ , and a best PCE of $19.82\%$ . For the devices based on ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ ETLs, the best PCEs based on the champion devices increase obviously, and the $10\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-TiO_{2}}}$ device shows the highest PCE of $21.33\%$ calculated from a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.19\mathrm{~V~},$ a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $23.40\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , and a FF of $76.60\%$ . It is noteworthy that this PCE value is close to the record PCE for devices with similar structures and fabrication procedures $(21.9\%^{[25\mathrm{b}]})$ . Moreover, such a high PCE can be stabilized at $21.14\%$ over the $200\mathrm{~s~}$ measurement period (see Figure  S11 in the Supporting Information). Besides, the $10\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-TiO_{2}}}$ device exhibits a narrower PCE distribution with the average PCE reaching $20.14\%$ , which is enhanced by $\approx7\%$ relative to that of the control device $(18.83\%$ , Figure  4d). Furthermore, in order to clarify the contribution of amino functionalization of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs via $\mathrm{Ti-N}$ bonds to the PCE enhancement, we fabricated control devices by incorporating urea as the interface layer between $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and perovskite layer, and found that incorporation of urea as the interface layer results in only slight changes on each photovoltaic parameters and the overall PCE (see Figure  S12 and Table S5 in the Supporting Information). These results confirm the necessity of chemical functionalization of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ by bonding the $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group via $_{\mathrm{Ti-N}}$ bonds.
On the basis of a detailed comparison of each photovoltaic parameter, the PCE enhancement of the device based on $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL relative to that based on the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ results mainly from the increases of both $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ (from 1.16 to 1.19 V) and FF (from $71.67\%$ to $75.19\%$ , whereas $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ exhibits negligible change (see Table  1). Such tendencies are further confirmed by a statistical study of each photovoltaic parameter based on 50 independent devices with different $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs fabricated independently (see Figure S13 in the Supporting Information). Specifically, the negligible change of $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ upon altering the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL is verified by external quantum efficiency (EQE) measurements, indicating slight changes on the overall curve and the integrated current density (Figure 4e).
As well known, the hysteresis of $J{-}V$ curves often exists especially in regular-structure PHJ PSC devices.[30] We examined the hysteresis of $J{-}V$ curves of our devices based on different $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs under reverse and forward scans (Figure  4f). The hysteresis index, defined as [PCE(reverse) – PCE(forward)]/ PCE(reverse),[31] decreases dramatically from $9.6\%$ for the control device to $1.8\%$ for the $10\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-TiO_{2}}}$ device (see Table S6 in the Supporting Information). Because the hysteresis of $J{-}V$ curves is mainly due to the existence of traps on the surfaces of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and perovskite layer as well as charge accumulation at the interface as reported extensively in literatures,[32] the dramatic suppression of the hysteresis of $J{-}V$ curves after amino functionalization should result from the reduced trap states and/ or effective electron extraction within the perovskite layer, as discussed further below.
Given that the defects at the surface of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film can be decreased by amino functionalization as concluded above based on the XPS analyses, it is necessary to investigate whether amino functionalization affects the trap states within the CsFAMA perovskite layer atop. We carried out a series of characterizations to probe the effect of amino functionalization on the charge carrier dynamics of the device. The electron trapstate density within the perovskite layer is measured by using space charge limited current (SCLC) method. An electron-only device with a structure of I $\mathrm{TO}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/($ sFAMA perovskite/ PCBM/Ag was fabricated, and the corresponding $I{-}V$ curves for the devices based on different $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs are presented in Figure  5a, from which the trap-state density $\left(n_{\mathrm{t}}\right)$ within the perovskite layer are calculated to be $7.17\times10^{15}$ , $5.37\times10^{15}$ , $6.67\times10^{15}$ , and $8.60\times10^{15}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ for $3\%{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}{\mathrm{-TiO}}_{2}$ , $10\%\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ - $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $20\%{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , and pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs, respectively (see Table S7 in the Supporting Information for detailed analyses). Thus, such a decrease of the $n_{\mathrm{t}}$ value by around $38\%$ for the $10\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-TiO_{2}}}$ device indicates the significantly reduced trap states within the CsFAMA perovskite layer. A plausible explanation for such reduced trap states is that the - $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups bonded onto $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs may coordinate via the lone-pair electrons of the nitrogen atoms with the $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions of the CsFAMA perovskite, fulfilling effective trap passivation at the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ perovskite interface (see Figure S14 in the Supporting Information for detailed analyses).[29a]
The effect of amino functionalization on the trap passivation at the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ perovskite interface is further confirmed by steadystate and time-resolved photoluminescence (PL) characterizations, from which valuable information on the charge recombination dynamics within the perovskite layer can be obtained.[33] Steady-state PL spectra of the CsFAMA perovskite films deposited on the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}–{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ layers measured under an excitation wavelength of $460\mathrm{nm}$ are compared in Figure 5b. Compared to the CsFAMA perovskite film deposited on the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer showing a characteristic PL peak at $755~\mathrm{nm}$ , the perovskite films deposited on the $3\%{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $10\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}}$ layers exhibit obvious PL quenching, suggesting more efficient electron transfer from the CsFAMA perovskite to ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}–{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ ETL benefited from the trap passivation at the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ perovskite interface.[33a] On the contrary, the PL peak at $755~\mathrm{nm}$ for the CsFAMA perovskite film deposited on $20\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}}$ layer becomes even stronger, suggesting inferior electron transfer from the CsFAMA perovskite to $20\%{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL due mainly to the mismatch of the energy level between the conduction bands of $20\%{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and perovskite as discussed below. The carrier lifetimes (τ) of the CsFAMA perovskite films deposited on different $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layers are further measured by the time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) spectroscopy, and the $\tau$ values based on fitting with a biexponential decay function are determined to be 113.79, 47.01, 64.36, and 162.80 ns for the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , $3\%{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , $10\%\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ , and $20\%\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ - $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ devices, respectively (see Table S8 in the Supporting Information for detailed analyses). The dramatic decrease of the $\tau$ value for the $3\%{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}{\mathrm{-TiO}}_{2}$ and $10\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-TiO_{2}}}$ devices relative to that for the control device suggests more efficient electron transfer from the CsFAMA perovskite to ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}–{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ ETL, as consistent with the steady-state PL results.
More direct insight into the recombination kinetics within the device can be obtained by measuring $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ at various light intensities.[34] Figure 5d presents the dependence of $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ on the incident light intensity (I) in logarithmic form according to $J_{\mathrm{sc}}\propto\mathrm{I}^{\alpha}$ , from which the slopes of the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , $3\%.\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , $10\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}}$ , and $20\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}}$ devices are 0.86, 0.89, 0.90, and 0.87, respectively. The slightly larger slope of the $10\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-TiO_{2}}}$ device than that of the control device based on the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ indicates that the bimolecular recombination during sweeping out the charge carriers is suppressed at the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ /perovskite interface.[34a] Besides, the dependence of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ on the logarithm of the incident light intensity $(\mathrm{Log(I)})$ is shown in Figure 5e, which can be fitted according to the equation $V_{\mathrm{oc}}=\varepsilon k T\mathrm{ln(I)}/e+$ constant, where $\varepsilon$ is the ideal factor, $k$ is the Boltzmann constant, $T$ is the absolute temperature, and $e$ is the elementary charge. A slope (ε) larger than 1 indicates the presence of additional trap-assisted Shockley–Read–Hall (SRH) recombination, and decrease in the slope indicates a suppression of the monomolecular SRH recombination, leading to improved device performance.[34b] The $\varepsilon$ values of the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , $3\%{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}{\mathrm{-TiO}}_{2}$ , $10\%{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , and $20\%{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ devices are 1.37, 1.21, 1.10, and 1.18, respectively. Apparently, for the $10\%{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ device, the $\varepsilon$ value is more close to unity, suggesting more effective suppression of the monomolecular SRH recombination. This is consistent with the results that amino modification results in trap passivation of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ perovskite interface as discussed above.
We further investigated the influence of amino functionalization on the interfacial charge transport behavior of the devices by using the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS). The Nyquist plots of devices based on different $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETLs measured in the dark under a reverse potential of $1.0\mathrm{~V~}$ (near the open circuit potential) are compared in Figure  5f, which also includes the corresponding fitted curves based on a commonly used equivalent circuit model shown in the inset.[35] The parameters used for fitting are summarized in Table S9 in the Supporting Information, among which the $R_{\mathrm{co}}$ (contact resistance) and $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ (recombination resistance) reflect the electron transport and the electron–hole recombination, respectively.[35] For the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}–{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ devices, the much smaller $R_{\mathrm{co}}$ values along with the larger $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ values than those for the control device are obtained, indicating that amino functionalization of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ enhances the electron transport and inhibits the electron–hole recombination at $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ perovskite interface. This can be understood since amino functionalization improves not only the conductivity of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL but also the interfacial contact between the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL and perovskite layer, beneficial for the interfacial electron transport. Besides, the reduced trap states within the perovskite layer may diminish the sites for electron– hole recombination. These joint effects contribute directly to the increase of FF value.
To understand the increase of the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ value upon amino functionalization, the energy level alignment between the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL and perovskite needs to be checked. We used ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) to probe the effect of amino functionalization on the work function of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . The determined work functions $(\mathbb{W}_{\mathrm{F}})$ of the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , $3\%\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ - $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , $10\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}}$ , and $20\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}}$ are 4.13, 4.10, 4.07, and $3.96\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively (see Figure S15 and Table S10 in the Supporting Information), revealing that amino functionalization leads to the decrease of the work function of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . The conduction band (CB) and valance band (VB) energy levels can be further determined, indicating that both CB and VB energy levels are raised to higher levels after amino functionalization (see Figure S17 in the Supporting Information). Previous study by Murakami et  al. revealed that raising the CB of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to higher level via $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ treatment resulted in higher $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ PHJ PSC devices.[36] Likewise, the raising of the CB energy level of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ after amino functionalization contributes to the increase of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ value in our case.
Finally, we evaluated the influence of amino functionalization on the ambient stability of the device by storing it without any encapsulation in ambient condition (temperature: $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ relative humidity: $35\%$ ) for $500\mathrm{~h~}$ . The control device based on the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is quite susceptible to the ambient environment, with the PCE dropping to $80\%$ of the initial value after $500\mathrm{~h~}$ storage, which is mainly due to the obvious degradation of FF as a result of the decomposition of the CsMAFA perovskite. Similar phenomena were reported in literatures.[37] After amino functionalization, the stability of the device improves obviously, and ${\approx}90\%$ retaining of the initial PCE is achieved for the devices based on $10\%{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL (see Figure S18 in the Supporting Information). The improved ambient stability after amino functionalization is presumably due to the surface trap passivation of the perovskite film as discussed above, enabling an enhanced resistance to the invasion of the moisture.[38]
We also tested the universality of our strategy in efficiency enhancement via amino functionalization of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL in another commonly used $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ $(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3})$ ) absorber system. According to comparison of the statistical data of over 30 devices, devices based on ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ ETLs all show higher average PCEs than that based on the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL, and the best PCE of $19.61\%$ is achieved for the $10\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}}$ device, which is improved by ${\approx}14\%$ relative to that of the control device based on the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL $(17.26\%$ , see Figure  S19a and Table S11 in the Supporting Information). Besides, the hysteresis index decreases dramatically from $24.2\%$ for the control device to $9.9\%$ for the $10\%\mathrm{-NH_{2}\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}}$ device (see Figure $\mathrm{{s19b}}$ and Table S12 in the Supporting Information). These tendencies are exactly similar to those of the CsFAMA devices, thus confirming the universality of our approach.
In summary, we have developed a facile one-step, low-temperature, nonhydrolytic approach to in situ synthesize aminofunctionalized $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right)$ NPs, and achieved high-efficiency PHJ PSC devices with PCE over $21\%$ by applying $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs as a novel ETL. By doping urea as a precursor during the alcoholysis of $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}.$ ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ NPs with amino $\left(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ groups bonding chemically onto the surface of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs via $\mathrm{Ti}{-}\mathrm{N}$ bonds are synthesized in situ, and show improved crystallinity with less defects relative to the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . Moreover, amino functionalization improves the interfacial contact between the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL and CsFAMA perovskite film, and consequently the crystallinity and phase purity of the CsFAMA perovskite film are improved, and the surface trap states of perovskite film are passivated due to the coordination between the bonded - $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups with the $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions of CsFAMA perovskite. As a result, PHJ PSC devices based on ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}–{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ ETL exhibit a best PCE of $21.33\%$ , which is significantly higher than that of the control devices based on the pristine $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL $(19.82\%)$ owing to the increases of both $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and FF, and is close to the record PCE for devices with similar structures and fabrication procedures. Besides, due to the passivation of the surface trap states of perovskite film, the hysteresis of current–voltage response is significantly suppressed with the hysteresis index decreasing dramatically from $9.6\%$ to $1.8\%$ , and the ambient stability of devices is improved upon amino functionalization. In situ amino functionalization method developed in this study is facile and effective in enhancing the device performance, promising its potential applications in low-temperature processed high-efficiency planar perovskite solar cells.
## Experimental Section
Materials: The ITO glass substrate with a sheet resistance of $10~\Omega/\mathsf{s q}$ was purchased from Shenzhen Nan Bo Group, China. All the chemicals were used as received, including formamidinium iodide (FAI, Dyesol), methylammonium bromide (MABr, Dyesol), lead iodide $(\mathsf{P b l}_{2},$ , $99.999\%$ , TCI), lead bromide $(\mathsf{P b}\mathsf{B r}_{2}$ , $99.999\%$ , Alfa Aesar), methylammonium iodide (MAI, Xi’an Polymer Light
Technology Corp.), $\mathsf{T i C l}_{4}$ (GR, $99.5\%$ , Aladdin), urea (Alfa Aesar), titanium(diisopropoxide) bis(2,4-pentanedionate) (TCI), spiro-OMeTAD (1 M company), and li-bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)imide (Li-TFSI, Sigma). Dimethylformamide (DMF), benzyl alcohol, chlorobenzene, and dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. All materials were used as received without further purification.
Syntheses of $N H_{2}{-}T i O_{2}$ NPs: The pristine $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ NPs were synthesized by using a modified nonhydrolytic sol–gel method with all procedures done in the ambient condition.[4a,8] First, $0.5\mathrm{\mL}\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ was injected drop by drop into $2m L$ cold anhydrous ethanol with strong stirring. After the solution cooled down to room temperature, $70~\mathsf{m L}$ of anhydrous benzyl alcohol was added to the previous solution and stirred for $10~\mathsf{m i n}$ The mixed solution was firmly sealed and stored without stirring in an oil bath pan at $85~^{\circ}C$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . The product of $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ NPs was then precipitated from the as-obtained solution by the addition of diethyl ether and isolated by centrifugation at 5000 rpm for 2 min. The solid was subsequently washed by adding anhydrous ethanol and diethyl ether, followed by a similar centrifugation step (5000 rpm for $2\mathsf{m i n}$ ) for three times.
The $N H_{2}{\cdot}T_{1}0_{2}$ NPs were obtained with a similar approach except that urea was doped into a ${\mathsf{T i C l}}_{4}$ solution dissolved in anhydrous ethanol as a precursor with variable N:Ti molar ratios of $3\%$ , $10\%$ , and $20\%$ . To stabilize the as-obtained $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ and $N H_{2}\cdot T i O_{2}$ NPs, the washed NPs were dispersed in anhydrous ethanol (concentration of around $5~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{mL^{-1}}$ ) by the addition of TIPD $(75\upmu\mathsf{L}m\mathsf{L}^{-1})$ .
Device Fabrication: A patterned ITO glass substrate was cleaned by sequential ultrasonic treatment in detergent, deionized water, acetone, and isopropyl alcohol. The as-prepared $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ NPs were spin-coated onto the ITO substrate at 5000 rpm for $40~\mathsf{s}$ for twice, and then these samples were annealed at $\mathsf{l}50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $25{\mathrm{~}}{\mathrm{min}}$ in air under ambient pressure. The following coating steps were performed under argon atmosphere inside a glovebox. For the fabrication of $\mathsf{C s}_{0.05}(\mathsf{F A}_{0.87}\mathsf{M A}_{0.13})_{0.95}\mathsf{P b}\mathsf{I}_{2.55}\mathsf{B r}_{0.45}$ (CsFAMA) perovskite, the CsFAMA precursor solution $(7.3~\mathsf{\ m})$ was prepared with molar ratios of $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}/\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}=\mathsf{I}.\mathsf{I}:0.2$ , FAI/MABr $=1{:}0.2$ , ${\mathsf{C s l}}/({\mathsf{F A l}}+{\mathsf{M A B r}})=0.05{:}0.95$ , $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}/\mathsf{F A l}=7.7{:}7$ , and $\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}/\mathsf{M A B r}=7{:}0.2$ . The perovskite films were deposited onto the $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ substrates with twostep spin-coating procedures. The first step was 2000 rpm for $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{10~s}}$ with an acceleration of $200\ r{\mathsf{p m\ s}}^{-1}$ . The second step was 6000 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ with a ramp-up of $2000\ r{\mathsf{p m\ s}}^{-1}$ . Chlorobenzene $(\mathsf{l o o}\upmu\mathsf{L})$ was dropped on the spinning substrate during the second spin-coating step at $\overline{{15\mathrm{~s~}}}$ before the end of the procedure. The substrate was then immediately transferred on a hotplate and heated at $\mathsf{l o o}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ , followed by cooling down to room temperature naturally.
For the fabrication of $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3}$ -based perovskite, a $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}/\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{N H}_{3}|$ mixture solution containing 1.1 m $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ and 1.1 m ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}|$ in $7m L$ of mixed solvents (DMSO/DMF, 3:7, $\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v})$ was prepared and stirred at $60^{\circ}C$ overnight. The mixture was then filtered and maintained at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ during spin-coating process. The solution was spin-coated at 3800 rpm for $20~\mathsf{s}$ . Next, ${\sf100\upmu L}$ of anhydrous chlorobenzene was then quickly injected onto the film and stayed for $3\:s$ , followed by spin-coating at 5000 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ again. The yellowish as-cast film was annealed at $\mathsf{l o o}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 10 min toward the formation of perovskite crystal film, followed by cooling down to room temperature naturally. After cooling down to room temperature, the hole transport layer was subsequently deposited on top of the perovskite film by spin-coating at $3000~\mathsf{r p m}$ for $30~\mathsf{s}$ using a chlorobenzene solution that contained $73.2\ m g\ m L^{-1}$ of spiro-OMeTAD and $28.8~\upmu\mathrm{L}~\mathsf{m L}^{-1}$ of tert-butylpyridine, as well as $78.8\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{\mL^{-}}}$ of bis(trifluoromethane) sulfonimide lithium salt $(520\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}}$ in acetonitrile). Finally, the device was transferred into a vacuum chamber $(70^{-6}$ torr), and an Au electrode $(\approx50\ \mathsf{n m}$ thick) was thermally deposited through a shadow mask to define the effective active area of the device $(0.70\mathsf{c m}^{2})$ .
Measurements and Characterization: The current–density voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ characterizations were measured by a Keithley 2400 source measurement unit under simulated AM 1.5 irradiation $(700\ m\mathsf{W}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-2})$ with a standard xenon-lamp-based solar simulator (Oriel Sol 3A, USA) that was calibrated with a monocrystalline silicon reference cell (Oriel $\mathsf{P/N}\ 9\mathsf{l}\mathsf{l}\ 50\ \mathsf{V},$ with KG-5 visible color filter) calibrated by the National Renewable Energy Laboratory (NREL). The measurements were performed with a mask (well-defined area size of $0.70\ c m^{2}$ ) to ensure accurate measurement in ambient conditions. Around 50 devices were fabricated and measured independently under each experimental condition to confirm the reproducibility of the result and to obtain the statistical histograms of the photovoltaic parameters. The EQE measurements were carried out on an ORIEL Intelligent Quantum Efficiency (IQE) 200TM Measurement system established with a tunable light source.
SEM measurements were attained via a field-emission scanning electron microscope (Zeiss Gemini SEM 500, Germany). AFM images were carried out on a XE-7 scanning probe microscope in noncontact mode (Park systems, Korea). XPS measurements were performed on a Thermo ESCALAB 250 instrument with a monochromatized Al $\upkappa\alpha$ X-ray source in vacuum. The XRD patterns were obtained on a Rigaku SmartLab X-ray diffractometer with $\mathsf{C u-K}\alpha$ radiation $(0.154~\mathsf{n m})$ . UV– vis spectroscopy was recorded on a UV–vis–NIR 3600 spectrometer (Shimadzu, Japan). The UPS experiments were measured at Ningbo Institute of Industrial Technology, CAS. The high-resolution synchrotron radiation photoelectron spectroscopy (HR-SRPES) experiments were performed at the Catalysis and Surface Science station in the National Synchrotron Radiation Laboratory (NSRL), Hefei. The spectra were measured by using synchrotron radiation light as the excitation source with a photon energy of $30{\mathrm{~eV}}.$ The steady-state PL spectra were recorded by employing an Edinburgh Instruments FLS920 fluorescence spectrometer with an excitation wavelength of $460\ \mathsf{n m}$ . The TRPL spectra were measured via the time-correlated single-photon counting method with a PicoQuant Gmbh Solea Supercontinuum Laser. A picosecond pulsed diode laser at $543n m$ with a pulse width of 104  ps was used as the excitation source. Impedance spectroscopic measurements (EIS) were performed in dark using an electrochemical workstation (Autolab 320, Metrohm, Switzerland) with a frequency range from 1 Hz to 1 MHz under 1.0 V. AC $20~\mathsf{m V}$ perturbation was applied with a frequency from ${\mathsf{1\ M H z}}$ to ${\sf1}{\sf H z}$ . The obtained impedance spectra were fitted with Z-View software $(\mathsf{v}2.8\mathsf{b}$ , Scribner Associates, USA). All the measurements were carried out in ambient surroundings. The GIXRD measurements were performed at the BL14B1 beamline of Shanghai Synchrotron Radiation Facility (SSRF) using X-ray with a wavelength of $1.\dot{3}8\mathring{\mathsf{A}}.$ . The 2D-GIXRD patterns were obtained by a MarCCD detector mounted vertically at a distance of around $274~\mathsf{m m}$ from the sample with an exposure time of 50 s at a grazing incidence angle of $0.2^{\circ}$ . The 2D-GIXRD patterns were analyzed using software Fit 2D and displayed in scattering vector $\boldsymbol{q}$ coordinates with $\begin{array}{r}{q=4\pi\sin\theta/\lambda,}\end{array}$ where $\theta$ is half of the diffraction angle, and $\lambda$ is the wavelength of incident $\mathsf{X}$ -ray.
